@section{Inheritance as Code Compression}

@subsection{Abstraction as Compression}

Inheritance of specifications, like function composition, macro expansion,
and actually all programming abstractions, can be seen as techniques for code compression,
from an information-theoretic point of view:
they enable programmers to describe the same algorithms with fewer concepts,
with fewer lines of code to write or read, and understand
(though the understanding will be harder if the abstractions are badly designed).
The effectiveness of various forms of inheritance can then be measured by
how much they will reduce the size of the code compared to a baseline of not using inheritance.
This effectiveness can be studied in practice or in theory,
comparing the compressed code to its expansion.
The practice would look at existing programs,
whereas the theory would model programs in terms of abstract parameters,
and look at the performance of the compression as these parameters vary
according to "realistic" values extrapolated from practice.

Now, the performance calculated by mindlessly expanding a program
would have to be moderated down by the fact that a manually written program
would not be so mindlessly written, but would instead do many mindful shortcuts and simplifications.
On the other hand, this performance should be moderated up by the fact that it enables
new kinds of programs that would just not be feasible if the programmer had to expand them by hand,
even with such shortcuts and simplifications.
By compressing the amount of information the programmer needs to process,
a superior programing abstraction (whether a form of inheritance or not) unlocks
programs that were not accessible before, and effects that were not achievable before.

@subsection{The Geometry of Object-Oriented Hierarchies}

We can extract from the Inheritance Hierarchy of a codebase or ecosystem
various quantities or distribution of quantities that describe this Hierarchy:

@itemize[
  @item{The total number C of concrete classes (or, outside OO, instanted types).
        Can go from a few tens in a small program to billions for an entire ecosystem@xnote["."]{
          Given a program design, C does not depend on the language used;
          but of course, languages will influence which designs are more affordable to develop.
          A non-trivial graphical application in a modern language (say JavaScript)
          will typically have 50 to 500 user-defined classes,
          maybe a bit less for simple applications
          based on frameworks leveraging higher-order functions,
          maybe a lot more (many thousands) for more complex applications.
          The total number of classes in Quicklisp is over 16000, plus over 2800 structs;
          many apps do with very few classes, but some big apps have thousands.
          Java has much wider adoption, and is very class-heavy, so numbers skyrocket,
          with several thousands of user-defined classes in a typical application,
          often hundreds of thousands including dependencies, and up to a billion defined total
          (early Dalvik VM limitations to 65535 classes were quickly a problem).
       }

}
  @item{The total number T of notional traits that are shared between concrete classes
        (actual traits with mixin inheritance or flavorful multiple inheritance).
        Also varies a lot, from tens to millions? Hard to tell at scale
        because of all the duplication between many programs and libraries.
        In all of Quicklisp, a bit under 19000 structs and classes.
        }
  @item{The size p(c) of the precedence list of each class c, and its average P.}
  @item{The size m(t) of methods for each trait t, summarized by its an average M.}
  @item{The number d(t) of direct dependencies to a trait
        (its parents in multiple inheritance), or D some kind of average.}]

Already, we can contrast the baseline size of code with no inheritance
vs the size of code in the best case of flavorful multiple inheritance:

@itemize[
  @item{
    With no inheritance, code size for all those methods has to be duplicated
    for each concrete class that shares those traits, for a total of @c{C*P*M}.
  }
  @item{
    With flavorful multiple inheritance, at the opposite end of the spectrum,
    these traits and their methods only take size @c{T*(M+D)} which is maximal sharing.
  }
  @item{
    With mixin inheritance, you have to specify the entire precedence list of every concrete class,
    not just the parents. I previously argued that is not modular.
    In terms of information, it expands the code size to @c{T*(M+P)},
    and @c{P} is larger than @c{D}, sometimes much larger
    (though in practice, D=2 and P=4 is a good overestimate of the average
    for a lot of Common Lisp code, but as we saw, d(t) can reach 61 and p(t) 63 in practice).
  }
  @item{
    Single inheritance is the same as flavorful multiple inheritance only in the case that D=1,
    at which point traits are ordered in a strict tree such that no trait ever needs be shared
    between disjoint trees. Otherwise, it's much worse:
  }

- The number of direct dependencies we will treat as a single typical number D,
  even though it's more of a distribution d(t), and the bottom of the dependency graph
  you have at the very least d-1 base traits with strictly fewer than d direct dependencies.
  In the vast number of cases, d is between 1 and 3, and we can use 2 as a "typical" value.



- By contrast, we may want to count total number T of traits in the same codebase.
  A trait is a recognizable systematic unit of behavior, that may be repeated
  many times over the program (in which case it is a fourth-class *design pattern*)
  or factored out into actual internal (first-class or second-class) entities.
  In an ideally expressive programming language using flavorful multiple inheritance,
  C is just the fringe of T, and so T>C (but not by much),
  and you never need to repeat yourself.
  In a horribly mismaintained code base in an inexpressive programming language,
  you might have C>T!, as the same traits end up endlessly duplicated and recombined
  in all possible permutations
  (and that's assuming we can summarize each combination down to the order in which
  each trait is used at most once).

- Similarly, we can consider the typical size P of the precedence list:
  how many traits a concrete class typically combines. Obviously D < P < T.

- We can also consider a factor I of interaction between traits, corresponding to how much
  the traits in a given precedence list tend to override each other's methods.
  I is a number between 0 and 1. When I=0, the traits are always independent from each other;
  when I=1, the traits constantly interact with each other.
  Reality is between these two extremes, and we can use I=.5 for back-of-the-envelope calculations.

Given these parameters, we can analyze how useful the various kinds of inheritance can be.

- Without any inheritance, every class must be written from scratch.
  The traits are fourth-class "design patterns" that programmers have to duplicate
  and repeat all over again. The total size of the code required is C*P*M.

- With single inheritance, you don't need to repeat traits as long as they are part of
  a shared line of ancestors. However, given the branching factor D, the shared line
  is typically of size S=(log P / log D).

considering some could have additional parameters, and further simplify by replacing each parameter by a single number representing its (weighted?) average, median, mode, maximum, etc., as appropriate depending on the context.

For example, with can call P the typical size
(obviously P < N, and for large enough applications, P << N).
We can call D the number of direct parents,
depth of the dependency graph between traits, number of parents for non-base classes, etc. We could e.g. have a random graph starting from 1 node, and each new node picks up to D random direct parents, removing those that cause an inconsistency or redundancy if added. Usually we have N >> D (N is many thousands in a rich Lisp application, D averages under 5 and maxes out around 20). We can also assume an average of M methods that matter per class; M is also relatively small, let's say it averages under 32.

With "flavorful" multiple inheritance, and its superclass linearization, you can indeed have each trait be a class, define each method once per trait, and have code of size Θ((C+T)*(D+M)), and it will automatically combine all the effects of all methods in a coherent order. That's the minimal code size, really.

Mixin inheritance requires Θ((C+T)*(P+M)) which is only a little worse, but the difference increases the deeper the OO gets.

At the opposite, with no inheritance, each combination of traits requires its own incarnation, which on average involves code of size Θ(C*P*M). That's making things worse by a factor N. That's much worse.

With single inheritance, any trait present in more than exact ancestry tail must be duplicated.
Sharing is proportional with the depth of the inheritance graph, as opposed to its total size.
Θ(C*P*M/log_D P)

Flavorless multiple inheritance is about the same as single inheritance, but
gets better if there is little interaction between traits (I small).

Other approach: with Flavorful multiple inheritance, you provide the minimum amount of information.
With Mixin Inheritance, you must specify the exact ordering of those classes,
which is about log(N!)~N*log N (length of N names) times the number of classes C
(in practice, we often say log N = Θ(1)).
With Single Inheritance, methods closer to the root are shared exponentially more,
but there are exponentially fewer of them. On average, we win a factor that increases slightly with P.
with Flavorless Multiple Inheritance - similar, but if I is small, closer to flavorful,
large closer to single inheritance.
}
